1. Microcontrollers
• STM32 Nucleo-F446RE

Main MCU

Runs the custom RTOS

Handles sensor sampling, control logic, and UART communication

• ESP8266 NodeMCU

Wi-Fi module

Hosts web dashboard

Controls relay via HTTP

Communicates with STM32 (UART or future expansion)

2. Sensors
• INA219 DC Current/Voltage Sensor (×2)

Measures voltage, current, and power consumption

One sensor monitors “Lamp Load”

One sensor monitors “Fan Load” (or second load)

I²C addresses used: 0x40 and 0x41

3. Actuators / Load Control
• 5V Relay Module (1-channel)

Controls power to LED strip (acts as the “Lamp”)

Active-LOW input

Relay input connected to NodeMCU pin D5

COM → LED Strip GND

NO → Power Supply GND

4. Loads (Devices Being Controlled/Measured)
• USB-Powered LED Light Strip

Acts as “Lamp Load”

Powered through INA219

Ground routed through relay to allow ON/OFF control

(Fan load can be added later, but LED strip is your current working test load.)

5. Power Supply & Wiring
• 5V USB Power Source

Powers the LED strip

Provides the load current measured by INA219

• Breadboard + Jumper Wires

Used for all connections between STM32, NodeMCU, INA219 modules, relay, and LED strip

• USB Cable for STM32

Powers and programs the STM32 board

• USB Cable for NodeMCU

Powers and programs the Wi-Fi module

6. Software & Firmware
STM32CubeIDE

Used for STM32 firmware development

Implements custom RTOS (round-robin)

Arduino IDE (for ESP8266)

Used to program the NodeMCU (dashboard + relay control)

Custom RTOS Kernel

Round-robin scheduler

1 kHz sensor sampling task

Communication task

Control task

ESP8266 Web Dashboard

Real-time visualization of wattage

Login system

Relay ON/OFF control
